\section{Wave timing of ordinary predictors}{
A bare covariate (\code{y ~ ar() + stress}) is measured at the \strong{focal}
wave -- the same wave as the outcome. \code{\link[=lagged]{lagged()}} is how a predictor at
the prior wave is requested (\code{y ~ ar() + lagged(stress)}); both are
expressible and neither is implied by the other.
}

\section{Moderation}{
A plain covariate, an \code{\link[=exposure]{exposure()}} term, or an \code{\link[=ar]{ar()}} term may be
moderated by exactly one plain covariate, written \code{*}: \code{exposure(x, via = w) * z} or \code{ar() * z}. This is hierarchical, the same rule \code{lm()} itself
follows -- \code{x * z} expands to \code{x + z + x:z}, and a bare \code{x:z} missing a
main effect is refused by name rather than silently fit. The moderator
must be numeric or logical (0/1); a factor moderator is refused rather
than dummy-coded or silently dropped. The \code{pooled} and \code{mixed} routes fit all
three moderands, and the simultaneous (\code{lnam}) route refuses a moderated
term (see Contemporaneous exposure).

A moderated term's effect size is a function of the moderator, not a
single number: \code{\link[=influence_effect]{influence_effect()}} reports, under
\verb{$exposures[["ex_..."]]$conditional} (and \verb{$ar} for a moderated \code{\link[=ar]{ar()}}),
the conditional estimate and a standard error that includes the
covariance term the two-term shortcut omits, at the moderator's 0, 10th-
and 90th-percentile values, plus a Johnson-Neyman crossing reported as
\emph{where the interval excludes/includes zero} rather than a significance
verdict. \code{summary()} states the conditionality: the coefficient printed for
the moderand is its effect \strong{at the moderator's zero}, and for a moderated
\code{\link[=ar]{ar()}} that it is not an unconditional stability parameter.

Moderation and a random exposure slope (below) are different claims and
compose rather than substitute for each other: a random slope says
susceptibility varies \emph{unexplained} across networks, moderation says it
varies \emph{as a function of z}. A moderated coefficient inherits every
endogeneity caveat the unmoderated one carried (see \code{\link[=netis_assumptions]{netis_assumptions()}});
moderation resolves none of them.

A moderator (or, for a plain-covariate moderand, the moderand too) may be
any ordinary column -- including one produced by \code{\link[=add_centering]{add_centering()}} or
\code{\link[=add_decomposition]{add_decomposition()}}. There is no separate argument for this: which
representation a moderated term uses is entirely a matter of which column
name it references (\code{ar() * z} moderates by the raw scale, \code{ar() * z_within} by \code{\link[=add_decomposition]{add_decomposition()}}'s within/actor-centered component),
since that layer never enters a predictor on its own. \code{moderation_provenance} (below) is where the fit
reports which derived representation, if any, was actually used. The same
\code{exposure()}/\code{ar()} moderand may not be moderated by more than one
moderator at once (e.g. simultaneously by both \code{z_within} and
\code{z_between}) -- refused by name, because the conditional-effect and
