\section{Wave selection}{
\code{waves = NULL} (default): with exactly two observed waves, both are used;
with one observed wave (or no wave column), the fit is a single
cross-section and only contemporaneous terms are available. With three or
more, rnetis refuses to choose a transition and \code{waves = c(from, to)} must
name one; one transition is fitted.

The outcome is measured at \code{to}, and every lag counts waves back from \code{to}
by position: a lag of 1 reads \code{from}, also when \code{from} and \code{to} are not
adjacent, so \code{waves = c(1, 3)} regresses wave 3 on wave 1 and wave 2 is not
read. A deeper lag steps back from \code{from} through the earlier observed
waves, and is refused when there are none. The fit reports as unused every
wave no term reads. A history mechanism such as \code{\link[=bi]{bi()}} accumulates through
every wave before the outcome, so it is refused on a transition that skips
waves.
}

\section{Contemporaneous exposure}{
\code{exposure(..., x_lag = 0)} measures exposure at the same wave as the
outcome rather than a prior one. Two cases are treated differently.
A contemporaneous exposure to
a \emph{different} variable (\code{content} is not the outcome) is fit as an
ordinary predictor, with a design diagnostic noting that temporal
ordering is not established. A contemporaneous exposure to the \emph{outcome
itself} -- \code{Y = rho * W*Y + X*beta + e}, an endogenous simultaneous-
autocorrelation term -- routes to \code{\link[sna:lnam]{sna::lnam()}} instead of ordinary
least squares or an unmodified mixed-effects fit, either of which would
be statistically invalid for this term. Requires the \code{sna} package.
Only one such term is supported (\code{lnam()} fits a single \code{W1} matrix),
and it cannot be combined with a \code{(1 | group)} random-effects term -- both are
refused with a clear error rather than silently approximated.

The term's \code{\link[=exposure]{exposure()}} options describe \code{W1}, and each is applied to it:
\code{direction} orients it; \code{w_lag} reads the network that many waves before
the outcome wave (the outcome wave itself by default); a derived conduit
such as \code{binarize(tie)} is resolved as for any other exposure; and
\code{normalize = "row"} divides each row of \code{W1} by its sum. Normalization is
applied after any actor with an incomplete row has been removed, so the
rows sum to one over the actors actually fitted. An actor with no ties then
has an all-zero row: \code{isolate_rule = "zero"} keeps it so (the actor
receives no contemporaneous influence), \code{"error"} refuses, and the default
\code{"na"} is refused because \code{lnam()} cannot fit a missing row of \code{W1}.
\code{mechanism} must be \code{"linear"}: any other mechanism computes an exposure
value rather than a matrix multiplying the outcome, and is refused. A
formula with a moderated term (\code{*} or \code{:}) is also refused on this route,
because in a simultaneous model a covariate's effect runs through
\code{(I - rho * W1)^-1} and a moderated coefficient is not its conditional
effect. For the same reason \code{\link[=influence_effect]{influence_effect()}} reports an exposure term on this
route as a coefficient-scale quantity that excludes that feedback, and rnetis
computes no quantity that includes it.

The fit reports whether \code{(I - rho * W1)} is invertible at the estimate.
The check is the eigenvalue condition: the operator is singular exactly
when \code{1 - rho * lambda = 0} for an eigenvalue \code{lambda} of \code{W1}, and
\code{summary()} prints the smallest modulus \verb{min |1 - rho * lambda|}.
}

\section{What is and is not identical to fitting by hand}{
The exposure columns in the fitted model are computed by exactly the same
\code{\link[=compute_exposure]{compute_exposure()}} engine documented there -- there is one exposure
resolution code path in the package, not two that could silently
disagree. Ordinary covariates are taken from the focal (\code{to}) wave (see
Wave timing above).
}

\section{Thresholds in the findings}{
A few findings in \code{diagnostics} fire at a number that is a package convention, not
a published cutoff: \code{too_few_groups_for_random_effects} below 5 groups for a random
intercept and 10 for a random slope, as a WARNING below half of that;
\code{near_zero_variance_component} when a component's standard deviation is under 1\%
of the residual's; \code{high_leverage_design_points} at a hat value above 0.5;
\code{structural_exposure_collinearity} at an absolute correlation of 0.9 or more; and
\code{insufficient_within_group_exposure_variance} at a mean within-group variance under
\code{1e-8}.
}

\examples{
nodes <- data.frame(
  group = 1, node = rep(c("a", "b", "c", "d"), 2),
  wave = rep(1:2, each = 4),
  y = c(0.2, -0.6, 0.5, -0.1, 0.4, -0.3, 0.7, 0.0),
  x = c(1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0)
)
ties <- data.frame(
  group = 1,
  source = c("a", "b", "c"), target = c("b", "c", "d"),
  wave = 1, tie = 1
)
nd <- netis_data(nodes, ties, wave_col = "wave")

# Two waves: the transition is unambiguous and need not be named.
fit <- fit_influence(y ~ ar() + exposure(y, via = tie), data = nd)
fit

# With more than two waves rnetis refuses to choose, and `waves =` names the
# transition. The fit then reports both what it used and what it did not.
nodes3 <- rbind(nodes, transform(nodes[nodes$wave == 2, ], wave = 3, y = y + 0.1))
ties3 <- rbind(ties, transform(ties, wave = 2))
nd3 <- netis_data(nodes3, ties3, wave_col = "wave")
fit_influence(y ~ ar() + exposure(y, via = tie), data = nd3, waves = c(1, 2))
}
